# Line-ending policy:
# - LF everywhere by default (what Git stores natively, and what every
#   non-Windows runner, linter and dependabot-style tool expects).
# - Windows-native file formats (Visual Studio projects, batch scripts,
#   PowerShell) keep CRLF so the tools that edit them don't re-save every
#   file with a whole-file diff on Windows.
# - Binary files are opted out of any text processing.
#
# This is enforced at checkout time.  Every text blob currently on
# develop is already stored as LF internally (verified with `git show`
# on each file), so switching the default here does not produce a
# mass-renormalization commit.  If a future commit reintroduces CRLF
# for a file not in the CRLF list below, the attribute will silently
# convert it back on the next checkout.

# Default: auto-detect text files, store and check out as LF.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Visual Studio solutions and project files must be CRLF so Visual
# Studio doesn't rewrite them on every save.
*.sln text eol=crlf
*.csproj text eol=crlf
*.vbproj text eol=crlf
*.vcxproj text eol=crlf

# Windows batch and PowerShell scripts expect CRLF.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.psm1 text eol=crlf
*.psd1 text eol=crlf

# Binary formats — never touched by line-ending normalization.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
